00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:05.020
Hello and welcome, I'm James Murphy and today
we're talking about the md5 hashing algorithm.

00:00:05.020 --> 00:00:12.290
md5 stands for "message digest 5" and what
it does is it takes in a message and outputs

00:00:12.290 --> 00:00:18.150
a summary, called the "digest", of the message.
Change the message by even one character and

00:00:18.150 --> 00:00:24.030
the digest is supposed to be completely different.
The message can be any length but the digest

00:00:24.030 --> 00:00:31.240
will always be exactly 128 bits, which we
think of as four 32-bit integers.

00:00:31.240 --> 00:00:36.920
md5 is what's called a "hash function", which
really just means it takes messages of any

00:00:36.920 --> 00:00:41.710
length to a fixed-length.
But md5 wasn't intended to just be a hash

00:00:41.710 --> 00:00:45.809
function, it was intended to be a secure hash
function.

00:00:45.809 --> 00:00:50.829
Among other things, you aren't supposed to
be able to find inputs with a given hash,

00:00:50.829 --> 00:00:56.970
or even two inputs with the same hash.
Unfortunately, md5 is not a secure hash function.

00:00:56.970 --> 00:01:01.880
It's completely broken for cryptographic use,
but it's still important from a historical

00:01:01.880 --> 00:01:06.850
perspective, so let's take a look at it, see
an implementation of it, and then discuss

00:01:06.850 --> 00:01:11.690
some real-world attacks against it.
But before we do, do you know who is not going

