Okay so let's just dive into the code.
What I have here is an implementation of the

00:01:39.829 --> 00:01:43.369
md5 algorithm.
The first thing that we need to do is decide

00:01:43.369 --> 00:01:46.110
what does md5 take in and what does it spit
out.

00:01:46.110 --> 00:01:49.880
So what I decided is that we're taking in
bytes and we're spitting out bytes.

00:01:49.880 --> 00:01:55.430
The way that we're going to do this is basically
we make a state object which is going to keep

00:01:55.430 --> 00:02:02.799
track of all the information of the algorithm,
we feed in whatever bytes that we have and

00:02:02.799 --> 00:02:08.220
then we're going to have to do some finalization.
And the reason for this is because md5 works

00:02:08.220 --> 00:02:18.020
on blocks that are 512 bits long, 64 bytes,
so if your input string was not the correct

00:02:18.020 --> 00:02:22.910
length that it has to get padded,
and that's going to happen in this finalization

00:02:22.910 --> 00:02:26.130
step.
So down here I have basically the same thing,

00:02:26.130 --> 00:02:29.209
but instead I'm directly passing in a file
handle.

00:02:29.209 --> 00:02:34.810
We see that the md5 state object is keeping
track of four things, the most important one

00:02:34.810 --> 00:02:40.290
is this state variable.
At the end, the state is going to be what

00:02:40.290 --> 00:02:46.989
is ultimately made into the digest.
The way that works is it operates on chunks

00:02:46.989 --> 00:02:53.739
of 512 bits at a time, so the first thing
is, of course, you need to take in exactly

00:02:53.739 --> 00:02:58.840
64 bytes,
and then we may convert those bytes to integers.

00:02:58.840 --> 00:03:03.870
So we take the whole chunk and we convert
64 bytes into 16 integers.

00:03:03.870 --> 00:03:07.650
Then we start with the process of updating
the state.

00:03:07.650 --> 00:03:13.709
So we have four variables, a b c d, that make
up our state variable then we're going to

00:03:13.709 --> 00:03:20.550
go through 64 times and basically mix in the
message in a particular way.

00:03:20.550 --> 00:03:27.909
So the general way that this works is for
each of the 64 steps there is a mixer for

00:03:27.909 --> 00:03:32.490
that step.
It's going to mix up bits and then we're also

00:03:32.490 --> 00:03:36.830
going to mix up which of the bytes that we
use from the message.

00:03:36.830 --> 00:03:43.110
The 64 steps of the algorithm are split into
four rounds of 16.

00:03:43.110 --> 00:03:49.110
In the first round we use the F mixer, in
the second-round we use the G mixer, third-round

00:03:49.110 --> 00:03:55.769
H mixer, fourth round I mixer.
So this mixer_for_step whatever is going to

00:03:55.769 --> 00:04:02.930
just tell you which of the F G H I is going
to be used for that step in the algorithm.

00:04:02.930 --> 00:04:10.030
Similarly we're not going to read the message
in order, and so in the first round you read

00:04:10.030 --> 00:04:15.790
the 16 integers in this order,
in the second round we read it in a different

00:04:15.790 --> 00:04:23.430
order, and so we take the mixer for a given
step and we take the index which integer do

00:04:23.430 --> 00:04:29.060
we want to read from the message at each step,
we just put both of those into big list so

00:04:29.060 --> 00:04:36.270
that for every step we know which int we're
supposed to be reading out of this array,

00:04:36.270 --> 00:04:39.010
and which mixing function we're supposed to
use.

00:04:39.010 --> 00:04:45.780
So for all 64 steps we pick the correct mixer,
we pick the correct index for the message

00:04:45.780 --> 00:04:49.419
that we're supposed to be using, and then
we just use this formula.

00:04:49.419 --> 00:04:54.680
We take a, and then we throw b, c, and d in
with the mixer and then add that in, and then

00:04:54.680 --> 00:05:01.210
we add the message at the specified index
in, and then we add in this sine randomness.

00:05:01.210 --> 00:05:05.080
This is just another thing that's just thrown
in just because the algorithm says to.

00:05:05.080 --> 00:05:11.550
Okay so back to the main loop.
We mix our bits, we rotate our bits, then

00:05:11.550 --> 00:05:18.639
we add a to b and then we rotate.
We take a b c d and you can see we take the

00:05:18.639 --> 00:05:23.800
d and we put it in the front here so that
we have d a b c.

00:05:23.800 --> 00:05:29.669
That's all there is to it. After we mixed
all of our bits up in this way 64 times,

00:05:29.669 --> 00:05:36.259
then whatever our state variables are, now
we add them to what they were at the beginning.

00:05:36.259 --> 00:05:40.949
So how do we ensure that we actually do this
for every 512 bits in the message?

00:05:40.949 --> 00:05:45.389
Well that's what the process function is for.
So here's what we do in a loop:

00:05:45.389 --> 00:05:50.120
We try to read however many bytes it takes
to fill up our buffer and make a full block_size

00:05:50.120 --> 00:05:53.669
number of bytes.
If our buffer was empty before and then I

00:05:53.669 --> 00:05:58.919
read in a full block size, then I just go
ahead and call compress on the buffer and

00:05:58.919 --> 00:06:03.259
then increase the length.
Otherwise I already had some bytes in my buffer

00:06:03.259 --> 00:06:07.050
or I didn't read enough bytes in order to
fill up the buffer.

00:06:07.050 --> 00:06:13.539
So what I do in that case is I note that,
you know, I've now filled up more bytes, and

00:06:13.539 --> 00:06:18.500
if I filled up, now, enough to do a compression,
if I filled up the whole buffer,

00:06:18.500 --> 00:06:22.260
then I go ahead and compress and increase
the length and then set the number of filled

00:06:22.260 --> 00:06:26.290
bytes back to zero.
Otherwise I still haven't read enough bytes

00:06:26.290 --> 00:06:32.470
in order to do another compression, so I just
keep reading bytes until I get to the end

00:06:32.470 --> 00:06:35.699
of the stream.
But what happens when I get to the end of

00:06:35.699 --> 00:06:40.970
the stream and I've read some bytes but not
enough to fill up the entire 512 bits to use

00:06:40.970 --> 00:06:45.550
a compression on it?
Well then we use this finalize method to basically

00:06:45.550 --> 00:06:50.979
pad the end.
What we have to do is we append one bit onto

00:06:50.979 --> 00:06:55.180
the end of the message and then we pad it
with zeros.

00:06:55.180 --> 00:07:01.880
In all cases we're going to pad with zeros
and append the length of the message that

00:07:01.880 --> 00:07:07.540
we just read as a 64-bit integer on to the
end of the message,

00:07:07.540 --> 00:07:11.980
but if adding that length on to the end is
going to overflow the block size, then we

00:07:11.980 --> 00:07:19.940
actually need to do an extra compression.
So we might have to add on more zeros, compress,

00:07:19.940 --> 00:07:24.099
and then add on the length, but in any case
we're basically just padding with exactly

00:07:24.099 --> 00:07:28.740
enough zeroes so that once we get to the end
of the message,

00:07:28.740 --> 00:07:35.650
we can add on our one bit, our zero padding,
and then our bytes representing the length

00:07:35.650 --> 00:07:42.710
so that it comes out exactly to a multiple
of 512 bits, and we do one last compression.

00:07:42.710 --> 00:07:48.020
Once we finalize the message then we just
need to output the digest or hex digest and

00:07:48.020 --> 00:07:52.870
the way this works is it's literally just
the state variables, whatever we updated them

00:07:52.870 --> 00:07:56.639
to,
that's now what we output as the digest.

00:07:56.639 --> 00:08:01.380
So why do we say that md5 is a completely
broken hash function at least for cryptographic

00:08:01.380 --> 00:08:04.900
purposes?
Well it's susceptible to a number of attacks

00:08:04.900 --> 00:08:10.379
and, for one, here is a collision.
Here's two different messages that have the

00:08:10.379 --> 00:08:14.979
exact same md5 hash.
I know they look very very similar, and they

00:08:14.979 --> 00:08:22.000
are, but they actually differ in two places:
there is 0x00 0x02 and 0x55 versus 0x55.

00:08:22.000 --> 00:08:25.949
They're a bunch of different types of attacks
against hash algorithms.

00:08:25.949 --> 00:08:30.340
If you say you have a collision attack against
a hashing algorithm, then you're just saying

00:08:30.340 --> 00:08:33.690
you're in the situation like I showed before
with these two messages,

00:08:33.690 --> 00:08:38.010
where are you just have two different messages
that have the same hash.

00:08:38.010 --> 00:08:41.580
And that's not necessarily that useful for
an exploit.

00:08:41.580 --> 00:08:47.780
What would be more useful to an attacker is
something like a chosen prefix attack.

00:08:47.780 --> 00:08:53.440
It's given this name because you can pick
any two prefixes you want like this one says

00:08:53.440 --> 00:08:59.790
"launch the nukes" and this one says "don't
launch the nukes" and then you can add on

00:08:59.790 --> 00:09:04.350
extra bytes, different sets of bytes to those
two messages, and then whatever suffix you

00:09:04.350 --> 00:09:09.120
want after that, such that those two things
have the same hash.

00:09:09.120 --> 00:09:12.910
Already in the real world there have been
several instances where either researchers

00:09:12.910 --> 00:09:19.860
or real hackers were able to use something
like a chosen prefix attack on md5.

00:09:19.860 --> 00:09:27.340
One instance they had a Microsoft code signature
for one of their binaries and they had Microsoft

00:09:27.340 --> 00:09:33.260
sign something that was completely innocuous,
but that completely innocuous thing had the

00:09:33.260 --> 00:09:38.450
same md5 hash as a virus.
So whenever you're allowed to control the

00:09:38.450 --> 00:09:42.800
prefixes like that, that's the case where
you can really start doing some damage.

00:09:42.800 --> 00:09:48.900
Okay so these last two attacks are just theoretical
for md5, there's no publicly known actual

00:09:48.900 --> 00:09:51.800
implementation that can do either of these
things.

00:09:51.800 --> 00:09:57.540
So a second pre-image attack is basically,
you have a file or message that already exists

00:09:57.540 --> 00:10:02.410
and you want to find another message with
the same hash.

00:10:02.410 --> 00:10:08.200
You can imagine, you have an operating system
a Linux image that lots of people are downloading,

00:10:08.200 --> 00:10:13.190
and hacker could switch it out for a different
image that has the same hash.

00:10:13.190 --> 00:10:17.760
And a full preimage attack is basically the
same thing but you don't even need the file,

00:10:17.760 --> 00:10:21.670
all you need is the hash of the file.
